 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity**: Sorting dominates. GPU multi-radix sort is $O(N \log N)$ with low constant factor due to parallelism. CPU sort is also $O(N \log N)$ but slower due to CPU-GPU transfer and less parallelism [S1, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for positions, keys, values, and histograms. GPU sort requires additional buffers for ping-ponging and histograms [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Time Complexity**: Sorting dominates [G].
+-   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for positions, keys, values, and histograms [G].
 -   **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
--   **Scaling**: Scales well with $N$ on GPU. CPU sort scales with CPU cores but is bottlenecked by memory bandwidth [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Scaling**: Scales well with $N$ on GPU. CPU sort scales with CPU cores but is bottlenecked by memory bandwidth [G].
 -   **Strengths**: Correct depth ordering for alpha blending; VR support; optional full SH rendering; flexible sorting backend [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
--   **Limitations**: Requires GPU for fast sorting (or falls back to slower CPU sort); 32-bit sort may be slightly slower than 24-bit but avoids artifacts; instanced rendering may have overhead for very small numbers of splats.
--   **When to Use**: When correct depth ordering is critical for alpha blending in 3DGS scenes, and VR or full SH rendering is desired.
--   **When Not to Use**: When performance is paramount and approximate sorting (e.g., screen-space order) is acceptable, or when hardware lacks GPU subgroup support and CPU sort is too slow.
